Objective: This 21 day email course is designed for the student to gain
a clear understanding of the credit repair process so that they can
approach the client file and create an action plan with certainty. The
content of this course is also designed to help the student effectively
communicate credit information so that as a business owner they can feel
comfortable and confident when speaking about the subject of credit to
leads, clients, and the general public.
Dispute 21 - Day 17

Dispute Preparation: 
Removing Personal Information  

I can't stress how important cleaning up your client's personal
information is before beginning the dispute process. In my opinion, this
step is just as important as disputing the derogatory accounts! Here is
why: the credit bureaus deal with MILLIONS of files daily. That means if
you have a social, your information is in their computer system. Do you
REALLY think the credit bureaus have enough time, let along man power to
actually investigate EVERY CONSUMER'S file that disputes an item on
their credit report??? Remember the credit bureaus don't even keep any
documents, original contracts, any type of proof in their system that
they can use to verify your accounts. So how can they verify your
accounts then? The answer is your PERSONAL INFORMATION.

You need to remove all of the old addresses, name variations, phone
numbers, etc off of your client's credit report.  Now with that said,
just because you update the personal information doesn't mean that all
of the derogatory accounts you dispute will just fall off, but it will
make the bureaus job a lot much harder and increase the chance to remove
any negative accounts with those old addresses. Removing old addresses
and incorrect personal information could boost your score up dozens of
points.

Here is some proof to why, if you haven't been doing this already, you
may not want to skip this step 

The top of the image is from the Derogatory Accounts section of a credit
report for a collections account with Advanced Collection Bureau. The
bottom of the image was captured from the top of the same report in the
Personal Information section. 

Notice how both "Address Identification Numbers" match. Coincidence huh?

Take a look at the Status Details. Could the address identification
match be the reason this item is still reporting? Maybe... 

Now, pay specific attention to the language used. Notice the remark says
"from our processing of your dispute..." I'm sure an investigation was
requested on this item, not for it just to be "processed." Remember,
above I told you that the bureaus sometimes verify accounts using
personal information. When this is done, an reasonable investigation to
ensure maximum accuracy is not performed. The dispute was simply
processed and verified.  

Below is another image showing an Address Identification Number Match-up
on a report that you may be a little more familiar with. This is an
image of the online Experian report. 

As we've learned in earlier lessons, the entire dispute process is
automated. Rarely are there human eyes that read your letters. Your
disputes are broken down into a two digit code. So, if the computer
notices a mismatch in the address ID then you are more likely to get the
account over to an actual person, sent through the proper verification
process or even removed. The same is true for a name change. If the
client's name has been legally changed and they've applied for credit
under their new name and the name is reporting on the credit report or
if they can provide proof of the name change and dispute an account
attached to their previous name, you are more likely to get the
derogatory information attached to the old name off the report.

When attempting to remove addresses from the report, there are a few
angles you can take. We'll discuss one here. If your client would like
to change their current address to an address that is not on the report,
you can ask the client if they have a relative with the same last name
who's address they don't mind their mail from the bureaus being
delivered to. In your update letter you could say that the client is
currently residing with their cousin or uncle and use their cousin's
or uncle's utility bill as proof of address, then request all other
addresses reporting be removed since they are not current and are
outdated. Because their last names match, it's easy to believe that
the updated address may be associated with the consumer, just like how a
utility bill will typically only have one name, but a married couple may
be living in that home. If you try this on a client, please remember to
inform them that their mail from the bureaus will go to that relative's
address.

If they don't want to send mail to a relative's address, a business
address, a customized UPS address or PO Box can work as long as they
have proof of the address with their name or someone of the same last
name. 

The whole point of this Pre-Dispute Process is to remove the Address ID
Code from the personal information section, so that it's less likely the
computers will find a match with a derogatory account and automatically
verify. When disputing the address, only list the physical address not
the Address Identification Number. Remember, the removal of an address
doesn't equate to an automatic removal of an account, although that may
occur. What you're wanting to do is make it harder for the bureaus to
verify so that they are either forced to conduct a proper investigation
as outlined in their procedures or delete the disputed account.

NOTE:
When reviewing the credit report with a client, always ask the client to
verify that they've lived or know someone who lives or may have lived at
each address on the credit report. You are doing this for two reasons.
(1) If there is an address that they don't recognize, an address from a
state that they've never lived in or even visited and it's not tied to
anyone that they know, it could be a case of a mixed file or potentially
a sign of Identity Theft or at least an attempt. (2) Some clients may
have open mortgage accounts at more than one address. You will want to
avoid disputing an address tied to an open mortgage account.      

